Die 1 is 4 and the other two dice are unknown, so the public fair value is 4 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 11. The distribution of the settlement is symmetric about 11, and so is the noise-trader valuation distribution.

I compared ask levels by expected profit per side, netting noise-trader gains against informed-trader losses (the informed trader buys when the settlement exceeds the ask). The net is about -0.1 at an ask of 11.5 and about +0.2 at 12.5. It peaks near 14.5 (about +0.46), with 15.5 slightly lower (about +0.44). The bid is the mirror image, so 7.5 is the matching bid. Position is flat, so there is no inventory skew.

{"bid": 7.5, "ask": 14.5}

At quote 5, die 1 = 4 and die 2 = 6 are known, and die 3 is uniform on 1 to 6. So the public fair value is 4 + 6 + 3.5 = 13.5. The informed counterparty knows the settlement S = 10 + die 3, which lies between 11 and 16.

Quoting a bid of 10 and an ask of 17 keeps the informed trader out, since it would sell to me only if S < 10 and buy only if S > 17, and neither can happen. Expected profit then comes only from noise traders, who trade with probability about 0.21 per side at these prices, each for an edge of about 3.5. Tighter quotes add more adverse selection than they earn in flow.

{"bid": 10.0, "ask": 17.0}

Die 1 is 5 and dice 2 and 3 are unrevealed, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. The sum is symmetric around 12 with a standard deviation of about 2.4.

Noise traders trade on the public value, so they cost nothing on average. Informed traders buy when the sum exceeds my ask and sell when it falls below my bid, which is adverse selection. Balancing the noise-trader edge against informed losses gives an optimal half-spread of about 3.8 from 12, with my position at 0. Rounded to the tick grid, that gives a bid of 8.2 and an ask of 15.8.

{"bid": 8.2, "ask": 15.8}

Die 1 is 1, so the public fair value is 1 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 8. Informed traders know the exact sum S, which ranges from 3 to 13 and has mean 8. Noise traders value the contract around 8 with standard deviation 3.

For an ask A, my expected profit is about 0.65 × P(V > A) × (A − 8) minus 0.35 × E[(S − A)⁺]. Evaluating this at several ask levels gives roughly +0.05 at A = 9, +0.23 at A = 10, and a peak of about +0.27 near A = 11. The bid side is symmetric around 8, so a bid of 5 earns the same expected edge.

{"bid": 5.0, "ask": 11.0}

Die 1 is revealed as 1, and die 2 and die 3 are unrevealed, so the public fair value is 1 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 8. The position is flat, so there is no inventory skew to apply.

Quoting at 8 + x on the ask and 8 - y on the bid, the expected profit on each side trades off noise-trader flow (which pays the spread) against informed flow (which picks off stale quotes). Working through the sum-of-two-dice distribution, the ask side is maximized near x = 3 (about 0.27 expected profit per side at 11, versus about 0.23 at 10 and 0.23 at 12). The bid side is symmetric, so y = 3 gives a bid of 5.

{"bid": 5, "ask": 11}

Die 1 is revealed as 1, so the public fair value is 1 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 8.0. With no inventory, I'll quote symmetrically around 8.

A half-spread of 3 (bid 5, ask 11) maximises my expected profit. Noise traders trade when their valuation (fair value ± noise with SD 3) crosses my quote, and each such trade earns me about 3 against fair value. Informed traders trade only when the true sum lies beyond my quote, which costs me on average. Wider spreads lose fewer informed trades but fewer noise trades, and the balance peaks near a half-spread of 3.

{"bid": 5, "ask": 11}

Dice 1 and 2 are known (1 and 6), so the settlement is 7 + die 3, uniform on {8,...,13} with public fair value 10.5. Only one quote remains, so this quote alone determines the outcome.

At a 13 ask and an 8 bid, no informed trader can trade profitably against me: an informed buyer needs V > 13 and an informed seller needs V < 8, and neither is possible. Only noise traders can trade. Their valuations are N(10.5, 3), so 13 and 8 are each about 2.5 from fair value. A quick check of nearby prices (for example 12.9 and 13.1 on the ask, 7.9 and 8.1 on the bid) shows neither improves the expected profit, so 8 and 13 are the best rounded prices.

{"bid": 8, "ask": 13}

The trade at 13.5 is strong evidence. A noise trader buying at 13.5 is unlikely (about 16%), while an informed buyer needs the sum to be at least 14, so the public fair value has risen from 10.5 to roughly 11.7. Quote 1's no-trade result barely moves the estimate.

With the informed trader (35%) trading whenever the true sum is beyond my price, my expected edge is roughly flat between asks of 13.9 and 14.5. Because the objective is expected profit, inventory doesn't change the per-trade edge, so I quote symmetrically around the fair value: half-spread about 2.5 gives bid 9.2 and ask 14.2.

{"bid": 9.2, "ask": 14.2}

Fair value is 4 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 11.0. Die 2 is still hidden, so the settlement S ranges from 6 to 16 and is symmetric around 11.

Against a 35% informed counterparty, each side's expected profit peaks where the informed buys or sells are rare but the noise trader still trades. On the ask side, selling at 14 gives about +0.27 per quote, versus about +0.23 at 13 and +0.25 at 14.5. By symmetry, a bid of 8 gives the same. My position of -1 doesn't change the expected value at a fair-value martingale, and it stays well inside the ±5 limit.

{"bid": 8, "ask": 14}

Die 3 is still hidden, so settlement is 8 + die 3, uniform on 9 to 14, with fair value 11.5. Fair value won't change before settlement, so the only way to profit is the spread. Informed traders only trade when the settlement value is on the far side of my quote, so I want quotes that capture noise-trader flow while limiting adverse selection.

Expected profit per side, weighting the 65% noise traders and 35% informed traders, is maximised near the symmetric points. The ask at 14.0 gives about +0.33 expected profit per unit of noise flow, and the bid at 9.0 gives the same. Position -2 is well inside the ±5 limits for the remaining quotes, so it doesn't change the choice.

{"bid": 9.0, "ask": 14.0}

Before any reveals, the fair value is E[sum] = 3 × 3.5 = 10.5. I quote symmetrically around it, at 10.5 ± d.

A noise trader buys at my ask with probability Q(d/4.4), so each noise trade earns about d in expectation. An informed trader only trades when the true sum lies beyond my quote, so the adverse-selection cost is small because the sum (sd ≈ 2.96) is narrower than the noise spread (sd 4.4). Summing the two effects over the 3-dice distribution gives expected profit of about +0.35 at d = 1, +1.09 at d = 3, +1.13 at d = 3.5, and +1.12 at d = 4. The optimum is around d ≈ 3.5, so I'll quote bid 7.0 and ask 14.0.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 14.0}

Die 1 is revealed as 3, and die 2 is not revealed until before stage 3, so the public fair value is 3 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 10. The informed trader's valuation is the settlement value S, which is symmetric around 10 with spread about 2.4. Noise traders' valuations are centred on 10 with standard deviation 4.4.

For an ask A, the expected profit is roughly 0.8·(A−10)·P(V > A) minus 0.2·E[(S−A)⁺]. Evaluating this gives about 0.58 at A = 13.5, with nearby values slightly lower. By symmetry the bid optimum is at 6.5, which gives the same value. This matches the quote posted at quote 4, so I'll keep it unchanged.

{"bid": 6.5, "ask": 13.5}

Die 1 = 3 and die 2 = 4, so the public fair value is 7 + 3.5 = 10.5. The settlement value S is uniform on 8 to 13.

At bid 7 and ask 14, an informed trader never trades, because it would need S < 7 to sell to me or S > 14 to buy from me, and S is confined to 8 to 13. Only noise traders can hit these quotes. Each side then earns roughly P(noise trades) × distance from fair value ≈ 0.8 × 0.21 × 3.5 ≈ 0.6 in expectation. Tightening either side to 13 or inside brings in informed flow, which reduces the expected profit. Widening lowers the probability of a noise fill faster than it raises the margin. The position is unchanged by this quote, so I'll keep the same symmetric quote.

{"bid": 7, "ask": 14}

The fair value starts at 10.5 (3 × 3.5). The sell at my bid of 8 is public information, and it makes a low total somewhat more likely. A rough Bayesian update puts the fair value near 9.7.

With nothing revealed, the uncertainty is about 3 points either way. The informed trader, who knows the total, makes wide quotes costly, so I'll quote a fairly wide spread of about ±2.9 around 9.7. I'm long one unit, so I'll keep both sides within the range and not skew the quotes much.

{"bid": 6.8, "ask": 12.6}

Die 1 is 5 and the other two dice are unrevealed, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. I'm long 1, so I'll shade both quotes down slightly to make it more likely I sell than buy. I'm keeping a spread of about 2.6, similar to recent quotes, to limit losses to the 20% informed traders.

Quote 6: bid 10.2, ask 12.8.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 12.8}

Die 1 = 5 and die 2 = 6 are revealed, so the public fair value is 11 + 3.5 = 14.5. The informed counterparty knows the sum is 12 to 17 (uniform), and noise traders value it at 14.5 ± 3.

Quoting bid 12 and ask 17 means an informed trader never trades against me, since no sum is below 12 or above 17. Only noise traders trade, and at these prices each side earns about +0.40 in expected profit. Moving either quote closer to 14.5 lets informed traders pick me off, and moving it further away loses noise flow faster than it gains per trade. The fair value doesn't change before quote 9, and my position is flat, so inventory doesn't shift the quote.

{"bid": 12.0, "ask": 17.0}

The public fair value is 5 + 6 + 3.5 = 14.5, since die 3 is unrevealed. This is the last quote, so inventory no longer matters beyond this trade. I'm maximising the expected edge from this quote's trades.

Against a noise trader (valuation about N(14.5, 3²), uninformed about V), each side earns its distance from 14.5 weighted by the fill probability. Against an informed trader (V uniform on 12 to 17, with probability 0.2), a bad fill costs the gap to V. Searching the 0.1 grid, the bid edge peaks at 12.0 (about 0.405) and the ask edge peaks at 17.0 (about 0.405). Both sides are positive, so I quote both.

{"bid": 12.0, "ask": 17.0}

Before any dice are revealed, the public fair value is 3 × 3.5 = 10.5. Noise traders value the contract at 10.5 ± 3, and informed traders know the exact sum (3 to 18).

I chose quotes by estimating expected edge per side, using the noise-trader flow and the informed adverse selection. The best ask came out near 13, and the symmetric bid near 8. The edge is flat around these levels, so the exact choice matters little.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 13.0}

The sum of three fair dice has expected value 10.5, and no die has been revealed yet, so the public fair value is 10.5. The previous no-trade outcome doesn't shift that: the posterior remains symmetric about 10.5.

Against noise traders, a quote at ask *a* earns roughly 0.8 · P(valuation > a) · (a − 10.5). Informed traders take the side that loses money for me, roughly 0.2 · E[(a − S)·1{S > a}]. Summing these gives about 0.31 at ask 12.5, 0.34 at 13.0, and 0.33 at 13.5, so 13 is close to optimal. By symmetry, a bid of 8 mirrors that ask around 10.5. I'll keep the same symmetric quotes.

{"bid": 8, "ask": 13}

With die 1 = 6, the public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. The settlement distribution is triangular around 13, ranging from 8 to 18. Position is flat, so I don't need to skew.

I chose quotes by maximising expected profit per side. For the ask, that is the noise-trader edge (ask minus 13, weighted by the chance they buy) minus the expected loss to informed buyers, who buy only when the settlement exceeds the ask. That objective peaks near 15.5, and by symmetry the bid is 26 − 15.5 = 10.5.

{"bid": 10.5, "ask": 15.5}

Die 1 is 6, and die 2 is still hidden, so the prior fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. The trade history suggests a higher value. The buy at 15.5 in quote 5 and the buy at 11.5 in quote 3 both point toward larger sums, and the no-trade result at quote 4 is consistent with that. A rough Bayesian update puts the fair value near 13.5.

With that estimate, I balance expected edge from noise traders (who trade on either side of fair value) against adverse selection from informed traders (who trade whenever the settlement value is past my quote). That gives a bid of about 11.0 and an ask of about 16.0. The position of -1 doesn't change the per-trade edge, since the position is valued at fair value.

{"bid": 11.0, "ask": 16.0}

Fair value before any reveal is the expected sum: 6.75 (die 1) + 6.8 (die 2) + 4.875 (die 3) = 18.425. The total standard deviation is about 6.5, similar to the noise traders' 6.5, so adverse selection from informed traders is substantial.

Balancing the spread earned from noise traders against losses to informed traders gives a half-spread of roughly 4. Rounding to the tick grid (bids down, asks up) gives bid 14.4 and ask 22.4.

{"bid": 14.4, "ask": 22.4}

Public fair value before die 2 is revealed: E[die 1] = 6.75, E[die 2] = 0.3·1 + 0.1·7 + 0.4·9 + 0.2·11 = 6.8, and die 3 = 3, so F = 16.55.

I computed the full settlement distribution and chose the ask that maximises expected profit from selling one unit. Noise buyers pay the ask against a value of 16.55, while informed buyers only take the trade when settlement exceeds the ask. The expected gain is roughly 0.5·P_noise(ask)·(ask − 16.55) − 0.5·E[(V − ask)⁺]. That is maximised near 23.6–24, and 23.7 is on the 0.1 tick grid. The bid is pulled at +3, so I'm quoting only the ask.

{"bid": null, "ask": 23.7}

Fair value before any reveal is the sum of the die means: 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.875 = 18.425. The total variance is about 42.6, so the sd is about 6.5, the same as the noise traders' sd.

Balancing adverse selection from informed traders against the spread earned from noise traders gives a half-spread of about 1.18 sd, or about 7.7. That puts the bid near 10.75 and the ask near 26.1. The previous quote produced no trade, which slightly trims the tails but barely shifts the mean, so I'll keep roughly the same quotes. Bids round down and asks round up.

{"bid": 10.7, "ask": 26.1}

The public fair value is the sum of the three dice means: 6.75 (die 1) + 6.8 (die 2) + 4.875 (die 3) = 18.425. Standard deviation is about 6.5.

Balancing noise-trader profit against informed-trader adverse selection, the optimal half-spread is about 7.7 on each side (the condition is roughly 2·Φ̄(z) = zφ(z), giving z ≈ 1.19). That puts the ask near 26.1 and the bid near 10.7. The no-trade history only slightly trims the tails, so I'll keep the same quotes as quote 2.

{"bid": 10.7, "ask": 26.1}

Expected settlement value: die 1 averages 7, die 2 averages 4.6, and die 3 averages 8, so the public fair value is 19.6. The two earlier no-trade quotes only mildly truncate the sum distribution and leave the mean at about 19.6.

Against a fair value of 19.6, the current quotes already sit near the expected-profit maximum. Fair-value-adjusted expected profit from the ask side is roughly flat between about 23.0 and 23.2, since the informed-buy set changes only at integer sums. On the bid side it peaks near 16. So I'll keep the same quotes.

{"bid": 16, "ask": 23.2}

Die 1 is revealed as 8. The unknown parts are die 2 (mean 4.6) and die 3 (mean 8), so the fair value is 8 + 4.6 + 8 = 20.6, with standard deviation about 2.96.

Using the exact distribution of the sum and the 35% informed / 65% noise mix, expected profit per quote is about 0.37 at a bid of 17 and about 0.33 at an ask of 24. Both are near the best available, and the position stays at 0.

{"bid": 17, "ask": 24}

Public fair value is 8 + 6 + 8 = 22, since die 3's mean is 8. Settlement is 21 (prob 0.5), 22 (0.25), or 24 (0.25). Noise valuations are N(22, 3.6²).

Each side's expected edge is 0.65 × P(noise trades) × (distance from 22) plus an adverse-selection term from informed traders. The informed term is zero for an ask above 24 and a bid below 21, since no settlement value falls outside those ranges. Both sides peak near 0.398 at a distance of 2.7 from 22, which gives bid 19.3 and ask 24.7. That is a myopic calculation and ignores inventory effects over the two remaining quotes.

{"bid": 19.3, "ask": 24.7}

Quote 2 is still in stage 1, so nothing beyond the mean is revealed. The mean of the sum is E[die 1] + E[die 2] + E[die 3] = 3.75 + 2.5 + 6.25 = 12.5, which is the public fair value.

With half the counterparties informed, a symmetric quote around 12.5 is adverse-selected. Setting the bid and ask to maximise expected profit (using the normal approximation with sd 5.8) puts the half-spread at about 6.9, so roughly 5.6 and 19.4. I'm long one unit, but holding inventory has no expected cost at a fair price, so I'm not skewing further.

{"bid": 5.6, "ask": 19.4}

Fair value before any reveals: die 1 has mean 7, die 2 has mean 4.6, and die 3 has mean 8, so E[sum] = 19.6. The total standard deviation is about 3.6, similar to the noise traders' 3.6.

Informed traders (35%) will hit whichever side is mispriced against the true sum, so I need a spread wide enough that noise-trader profit covers adverse selection. Balancing the two, the expected profit per side peaks at roughly 3.3 to 3.7 from fair value, and I'll use about 3.4 on each side. That puts the ask at 23.0 and the bid at 16.2, both on the 0.1 grid.

{"bid": 16.2, "ask": 23.0}

Public fair value is 27: die 1 = 11, die 2 = 8, and die 3 has mean 8. The informed counterparty knows the exact sum, which is 26 (prob 1/2), 27 (1/4) or 29 (1/4).

I quoted symmetrically around 27 and chose each side to maximise expected profit against the mix of informed and noise traders. On the ask side, the best point is about 29.7, where the noise-trader gain is largest and the informed trader rarely buys. On the bid side, the mirror point is about 24.3. The position of -2 doesn't change the expected-value calculation because the objective is risk-neutral.

{"bid": 24.3, "ask": 29.7}

The public fair value is 16: the means of the three dice are 5.75, 5.00 and 5.25. Noise traders value the contract around 16 with sd 6.6, and about 20% of arrivals know the exact settlement value. I'm treating each quote as a single-step pricing problem and ignoring how later trades update the fair value.

A zero-expected-profit spread would be only about ±1.2 around 16, but that gives no edge. Maximising expected profit on this quote means balancing the margin earned from noise traders against the expected loss to informed buyers and sellers. That balance comes out near ±6, so I'll keep the same quote as before.

{"bid": 10, "ask": 22}

Fair value before this quote is E[die 1] + E[die 2] + 7 = 5.75 + 5 + 7 = 17.75. The settlement has standard deviation about 5.0, and the noise-trader valuation spread is 6.6.

I set each side to maximise expected edge per quote: noise-trader profit minus expected adverse-selection loss from the 20% informed flow. The ask edge peaks near 23.5 (about 0.80), and the bid edge peaks near 12 (about 0.83). Inventory is +1, which doesn't change expected value under a risk-neutral objective, and the limits are not close to binding.

{"bid": 12, "ask": 23.5}

Fair value before any reveal: E[die1] = 5.75, E[die2] = 5.00, E[die3] = 5.25, so E[sum] = 16. The sum's standard deviation is about 6.6, close to the noise trader's 6.6.

Quoting at 16 loses money to informed flow, so I widen the spread. For a one-sided ask at 16 + d, the expected profit per quote is roughly d·(1 − Φ(d/σ)) − 0.2·σ·φ(d/σ). Setting the derivative to zero gives d/σ ≈ 0.88, so d ≈ 5.8. The bid is symmetric. That puts the ask near 21.8 and the bid near 10.2.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 21.8}

**Fair value:** Die 3 = 6 is revealed. Die 1 has mean 5.75 and die 2 has mean 5, so the expected settlement is 5.75 + 5 + 6 = **16.75**. The settlement standard deviation is about 5.0, which is smaller than the noise traders' 6.6.

**Spread:** Quoting roughly 5.6 either side of fair value (bid 11.1, ask 22.4) approximately maximises expected profit per quote, balancing noise-trader flow against adverse selection from the 20% informed traders. The position of -1 is small and does not affect expected settlement profit, so I'm not skewing the quote.

{"bid": 11.1, "ask": 22.4}

Since die 3 = 6 and die 1 = 7 are revealed, the unrevealed die 2 gives V = 13 + D2, with D2 in {1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 12}. The earlier trades (buys at 21.7 and 22.5, with no trades at lower asks) make D2 = 12 (V = 25) somewhat more likely than the others. The posterior is roughly 0.21 for V = 25 and about 0.16 for each of the other five values, so the expected value is about 18.4.

Noise-trader valuations are centred on about 18.4 with standard deviation 6.6. Informed traders only trade against me when V is outside my quote. Maximising expected profit on each side separately gives a bid around 14.2 and an ask around 24.5. The ask is high because an informed buyer would only take it at V = 25, and the noise-trade gain outweighs that loss.

{"bid": 14.2, "ask": 24.5}

Fair value is 7 + 6 + E[die 2] = 13 + 5 = 18. Noise traders are centred on 18 with sd 6.6, and informed traders value the contract at 14, 15, 16, 17, 21 or 25.

Since noise trades are independent of the settlement value, each noise trade earns (quote - 18) on the ask side and (18 - quote) on the bid side. Informed traders only trade against the quote when it is unprofitable for us, so wide quotes capture most of the edge. Maximising expected profit per side gives an ask near 23.5 and a bid near 13.1, each worth about 0.84 and 0.90 in expectation. My position of -2 does not change expected profit, so I'm not skewing either side for inventory.

{"bid": 13.1, "ask": 23.5}

Fair value given public info: die 1 has mean 8 and die 2 has mean 16/3 ≈ 5.33 (32/6), and die 3 is known to be 9. So the public fair value is about 22.3, with a standard deviation near 4.4 from the two unknown dice.

Against this, an informed counterparty (35%) trades only when the true sum is beyond my quote, which costs me the gap. A noise trader (65%) trades when its valuation, with sd 5.7, is beyond my quote, and each such trade earns me my distance from fair value. Balancing these, the expected profit per side is roughly flat between 4.5 and 6 points from fair value and peaks near 5.5. That puts the quotes near 16.8 and 27.8. The current 14.1/25.6 is too tight on the ask and too wide on the bid.

{"bid": 16.8, "ask": 27.8}

Fair value is E[S] = E[die 1] + E[die 2] = 5 + 6.5 = 11.5. Nothing about die 2 is revealed yet, so the public value is 11.5.

With a 50% informed share, the spread has to cover adverse selection. Since the sum is bimodal (die 2 is 10 or 11 about half the time), buyers are likely informed when the sum is high. Working through the zero-profit conditions for a single trade gives roughly an ask near 14.2 and a bid near 8.0. I'm short one unit, so I'll nudge the bid up slightly to make buying back easier and the ask up slightly to avoid adding to the short.

{"bid": 8.2, "ask": 14.4}

The expected settlement is 11.5 before any information (die 1 averages 5, die 2 averages 6.5). The quote 1 no-trade result shifts this slightly, to roughly 11.8, because the informed trader would have traded if the value were outside [5, 17].

Die 2 is bimodal (mostly 0 or 10), so the value is very uncertain and adverse selection from informed traders is large. Balancing expected noise-trader profit against informed-trader losses, the best ask is about 17 and the best bid is about 6. The spread stays wide because die 2 is not revealed until after this quote.

{"bid": 6.0, "ask": 17.0}

Fair value: the remaining 51 cards sum to 355, so card 2 and card 3 each average about 6.96. That gives 9 + 2 × 6.96 ≈ 22.92.

I set each side's quote where the marginal gain from widening it equals the marginal loss to informed traders. That puts the ask about 6.85 above fair value and the bid about 6.85 below, which gives roughly 29.8 and 16.0 after rounding.

{"bid": 16.0, "ask": 29.8}

Card 3 is unrevealed, and the remaining 50 cards average 7, so the public fair value is 9 + 5 + 7 = 21. Informed buyers trade only when the true value (14 + card 3, ranging over 15 to 27) is beyond my ask or below my bid. Noise buyers and sellers trade against the fair value of 21 with standard deviation 6.4.

Choosing the ask to maximise expected profit, the marginal condition P(V > a) + P(X > a) = (a − 21)·f_X(a) is satisfied just at a = 27, the point where the informed buyer's value of 27 stops qualifying. On the bid side the same condition puts the optimum at b = 15, where the informed seller's value of 15 stops qualifying. Both quotes sit at the edge of the informed-trader support, so the adverse-selection cost is zero and each noise trade earns 6 in expectation.

{"bid": 15, "ask": 27}

The public fair value is the mean of the sum: about 18.92, since each die's mean is 6.33, 6.25 and 6.33. Nothing has been revealed yet, so this is the best estimate of settlement.

Informed counterparties only trade against me when the true sum falls outside my quotes. The probability of a sum at or above 29 is 3/36 ≈ 0.083, and the probability of a sum at or below 9 is 12/144 ≈ 0.083. These tail probabilities match. Balancing the noise-trader edge against adverse selection puts the optimal half-spread near 9.7, which gives a bid near 9.2 and an ask near 28.7. The previous quotes already sit at this level, so I'll keep them.

{"bid": 9.2, "ask": 28.7}

Die 3 = 7 and die 1 = 2 are revealed, so the settlement value is 9 + die 2, where die 2 is 1, 2, 11 or 11 with equal probability. That gives V ∈ {10, 11, 20} with probabilities 1/4, 1/4, 1/2. The public fair value is 9 + 6.25 = 15.25.

An informed counterparty only buys from me if my ask is below V, and only sells to me if my bid is above V. With the ask above 20 and the bid below 10, no informed trade is possible, so I face only noise traders (valuation ~ N(15.25, 9.8)). Their expected trade profit is maximised at about 7.36 from fair value on each side, which gives a bid near 7.9 and an ask near 22.6. Rounding bids down and asks up gives 7.8 and 22.7.

{"bid": 7.8, "ask": 22.7}

Before any cards are revealed, the fair value of the sum is 3 × 7 = 21. The sum's standard deviation is about 6.3 (sampling without replacement), and noise valuations are N(21, 4.8).

Quoting symmetrically around 21 and widening the spread, I estimated the expected profit per side as 0.8 × P(noise trade) × edge minus 0.2 × E[informed adverse selection]. That net is roughly zero at a half-spread of 1, about +0.2 at 2, and peaks near +0.45 around a half-spread of 4.5. So I'm quoting a wide, symmetric market at 16.5 / 25.5. This ignores inventory risk and the information that later reveals will bring.

{"bid": 16.5, "ask": 25.5}

With no information revealed, the fair value is 21: each card averages 7, so three cards average 21. The settlement standard deviation is about 6.5 (each card has variance 14, so the sum has variance 42).

Half of counterparties are informed and will only trade on the side where the settlement value is beyond my quote. That adverse selection favours a wide spread. Balancing my expected spread income from noise traders against expected losses to informed traders gives an optimal half-spread of about 7.7. Rounding bids down and asks up gives a bid of 13.3 and an ask of 28.7.

{"bid": 13.3, "ask": 28.7}

The revealed cards are 8 and 12, so the public fair value is 8 + 12 + E[card 3] = 20 + 344/50 = 26.88. Card 3 is uniform over the 50 remaining cards, which have values summing to 344.

Quoting about 5 away from fair value on each side roughly maximises expected profit against noise traders, and the informed trader's adverse selection is small at that width. At an ask of 31.9, an informed buyer would only trade if card 3 were 13, so the risk is low. The bid at 21.8 is similarly safe, since an informed seller would need card 3 to be 1. Rounding follows the rules: the bid rounds down and the ask rounds up.

{"bid": 21.8, "ask": 31.9}

Card 1 is a 2, so the remaining 51 cards sum to 362 and each unrevealed card has mean 362/51 ≈ 7.10. The fair value of the settlement is 2 + 2 × 7.10 ≈ 16.2.

